insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('dreaming-about-someone-who-died', 'Dreaming About Someone Who Died', 'They were alive in the dream, and then you woke up. Why these dreams come, why waking from them hurts so much, and what to do with the hour afterward.', 'In the dream they were just there. Making tea, sitting in the passenger seat, saying something ordinary. Nothing about it was strange while it was happening. Then you woke up, and for a second or two you did not know, and then you did.

That drop is one of the cruelest parts of grief, and almost nobody warns you about it. Some mornings you lose them again before you have even got out of bed.

Why these dreams come

Sleep is partly where your mind sorts through what it has been carrying. When somebody central to your life is gone, that is the largest thing you are carrying, so it shows up at night whether or not you have been thinking about them during the day.

The dreams tend to come in waves rather than steadily. Often more in the first months, then in bursts around anniversaries, birthdays, holidays, a house move, or any time life changes in a way you would have told them about.

What the dreams are like varies enormously and none of it means anything is wrong with you. Some people dream the person alive and well and perfectly ordinary. Some dream the illness or the accident over and over. Some dream they are searching for them through rooms and never find them. Some dream nothing at all for a year and then dream vividly. Some people never dream of them and feel cheated by that, which is its own kind of ache.

Why waking up hurts so much

For a moment after waking, the part of you that keeps track of what is true has not caught up. So the loss arrives fresh, with the full force it had at the beginning. People describe it as going back to day one.

That does not mean you are back at day one. Grief does not undo its own progress overnight. What has happened is that you had a few seconds without the knowledge, and then the knowledge returned, and returning is painful in a way that simply knowing is not.

Give the first hour some room

The hour after one of these dreams deserves gentler handling than you are probably giving it.

Do not jump straight to the phone and the day. Sit up. Put your feet on the floor. Have something warm. Let the tears come if they are there, and let them not come if they are not, since plenty of people wake from these dreams dry-eyed and shaken.

Say out loud what is true: it is Thursday, they died in March, I dreamed about them. Naming the facts does not take the sadness away. It stops the confusion from making everything worse.

If you can, tell someone. A text to a sibling or a friend saying I dreamed about Dad last night does more than you might think, mainly because it stops you carrying the morning alone.

What to do with what the dream contained

It is tempting to read these dreams as messages, and whether you take them that way is yours to decide. There is nothing foolish about finding comfort in one. There is also nothing missing in you if you find them simply painful.

What is usually more useful than interpreting the dream is noticing what it stirred up. A dream where you argued may point at something unresolved. A dream of searching often arrives when you are feeling the absence most practically. A dream of them well and happy can leave behind a longing that is worth letting yourself feel rather than shaking off.

Writing a few lines about it helps some people. Not an analysis, just what happened and how it left you. Over months, those notes often show the dreams softening, which is hard to see from inside one morning.

The dreams that keep you from sleeping

If a dream of the death itself keeps repeating, or you are frightened to fall asleep, that is a different thing and it is treatable. Repeating distressing dreams respond well to specific approaches a therapist can teach, and you do not have to put up with them for years.

The same is true if you are waking at the same time every night, drinking to get to sleep, or lying awake dreading the dream. Those patterns tend to build on themselves, and they are worth mentioning to a doctor sooner rather than later.

When the dreams change

Many people find that over a long time the quality shifts: less searching, less of the hospital, more of the person as they were. That shift is not something you can hurry, and it is not a sign of forgetting. People worry about that a great deal, as though sleeping peacefully were a kind of disloyalty. Carrying someone well and sleeping well are not opposites.

When to get help

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if the dreams are keeping you from sleeping, if you dread the night, if you are using alcohol to get through it, or if months on the grief is as raw as the first week and you cannot function in your days. Grief that stays stuck has good treatments, and so do nightmares. If you ever feel you cannot go on without them, please tell someone today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time. Dreaming about someone you loved is not a problem to be fixed. The exhaustion around it sometimes is, and that part can be helped.', array['Grief', 'Loss']::text[], 0, '2026-10-22T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-night-before-an-operation', 'The Night Before an Operation', 'You are not allowed to eat, you cannot settle, and every thought goes to the worst version. How to get through the evening before surgery and the hours of waiting the next morning.', 'The bag is packed. You have the time you are meant to arrive and the hour after which you cannot eat or drink. And now there is a whole evening to fill, with nothing to do but think about it.

It is a strange kind of waiting. You are not ill right now, in this chair. You are simply holding the knowledge of tomorrow, and your mind keeps running the frightening version while your body gets ready for something it cannot do anything about.

Why the night before is harder than the day

On the day there are nurses, forms, a gown, people telling you where to go. There is movement, and movement uses up the fear.

The night before has none of that. There is nothing to do, which leaves your attention free to run ahead. Add hunger from the fasting rules, a disrupted routine, and a very early alarm set for the morning, and a bad night is close to guaranteed. That is the situation, not a failure of nerve on your part.

Deal with the practical list first

A surprising amount of the churning is admin your mind has not been able to put down. Write it out and it stops circling.

The address and the time, written where you will see it. Who is taking you and who is collecting you. Your medication list and whether you have been told to stop any of them. Bag packed: phone charger with a long cable, loose clothes, slippers, something to read, lip balm, earplugs, a list of questions. Who is feeding the cat, collecting the children, telling your work. Where your glasses, hearing aids or dentures will be while you are under.

Once the list is written, you are allowed to tell yourself the arrangements are handled, because they are.

Ask the questions you have been afraid to ask

People often go into surgery carrying a specific fear they never said out loud: that they will wake up during it, that they will be sick afterward, that the pain will not be controlled, that they will say something embarrassing, that they will not wake up at all.

Ask. The anesthesiologist usually sees you beforehand and these are the most ordinary questions they hear. Getting a plain answer about how they manage pain, or sickness, or what they monitor while you are asleep, settles more than any amount of reassurance from the internet, and it hands you a fact instead of a fantasy.

Stop looking it up

By the night before, searching has no upside left. You are not going to learn anything that changes the plan, and search results are full of the rare bad outcome, because that is what people write about. If your hand keeps reaching for the phone, put it in another room and tell the person with you why.

Let the evening be dull and occupied

The aim is not calm. The aim is occupied. Something absorbing and undemanding: an episode of something you have seen before, a jigsaw, a hot shower, a walk around the block, folding laundry, a call with someone who will talk about their own life rather than yours.

Keep the night before free of big conversations, difficult relatives and anything you have to decide. Tomorrow will take what you have.

Expect a poor night and plan for it

Lying there not sleeping is miserable mostly because of what you tell yourself about it: that you will be exhausted, that you cannot face it tired. One bad night before a procedure is normal and the team is used to it. Your body will do what it needs to do under anesthetic whether or not you slept.

Make the night itself easier where you can. Get into bed late rather than early, so you are not lying awake for hours. Keep the room cool and dark. If you are still awake after a long stretch, get up, sit somewhere dim, do something quiet, go back when you are heavy. If your mind reaches for the surgery, give it something duller and more physical: the weight of the blanket, your breath going out slowly, counting backward from three hundred in sevens.

The waiting on the day

The morning often involves hours of waiting in a gown, and that stretch catches people out. Bring something for it. Ask the nurses roughly when you are on the list, and ask again if the time passes, because being told you are third is easier than wondering.

If the fear peaks while you wait, the quickest help is cold and breath: a cold cloth on your wrists or neck, and letting each breath out slower than you take it in. Tell a nurse you are frightened. They have something for that, and saying it out loud takes the edge off.

When to get help

Tell the team beforehand if you have had a bad reaction to anesthesia, if needles or enclosed spaces frighten you badly, or if you have had panic attacks, because there are things they can do differently when they know. Please talk to your doctor if dread of a procedure is strong enough that you are considering canceling, or if it has you unable to sleep or eat for days, and ask about speaking to a therapist. Being frightened before an operation is ordinary. Being frightened alone is not necessary.', array['Worry', 'Sleep']::text[], 0, '2026-10-22T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-check-up-you-have-been-avoiding', 'The Check-Up You Have Been Avoiding', 'The letter came months ago and the appointment still has not been made. Why avoidance grows the longer it goes on, and how to get the call made without a lecture from yourself.', 'There is a letter somewhere in the house. A screening invitation, a dental reminder, a note saying you are due for bloods, a referral you never followed up. It has been there long enough that you have stopped seeing it, and every so often you remember and feel a small drop in your stomach.

Nothing about this is a mystery to you. You know it should be done. Knowing is not the part that is missing.

Why it gets harder rather than easier

Avoidance works in the short run, which is exactly the problem. Each time you decide not to deal with it, you get a small immediate relief, and that relief teaches your brain that not dealing with it is the move that helps.

Then the delay itself becomes a second reason to avoid. Now there is the appointment to face and the awkwardness of how long you left it. People go years on that loop, and the longer it runs the more certain they become that the reception desk will judge them, which nobody there will.

There is often a third layer: the fear of what might be found. If you never go, you never get told, and a part of you treats that as safety. It is worth naming plainly, because it is usually the real reason, and it is the one piece of this that avoidance makes genuinely worse.

Take the shame out of the way first

The story people carry is some version of: a person who looks after themselves would have done this ages ago, so what does it say about me.

It says you are a person who finds a particular thing frightening or boring, and who has been putting it off, which is most people. Dentists, nurses and doctors see long gaps constantly. The gap is not what they are interested in. What happens next is.

That matters practically, not just emotionally, because shame keeps the avoidance running. Someone who feels bad about themselves for not having called is more likely to keep not calling, so softening the story is part of the mechanics, not a nicety.

Shrink the step until it is nearly nothing

Make the appointment is not one task. It is find the letter, find the number, work out the opening hours, be free when they are, get through, speak to a person, find a date, write it down. No wonder the day goes by.

So pick the smallest piece and do only that. Find the letter today and leave it on the counter. Save the number in your phone. Open the booking page and close it again. Book online if that is possible, since a form is far easier than a conversation when dread is the obstacle.

Attach it to something you are already doing. Phone it on the way home from the store. Book it while the kettle boils. People who wait to feel ready wait a long time, and the readiness usually arrives a minute after starting, not before.

Borrow somebody

Say out loud to one person that you are going to call tomorrow. Ask them to ask you about it. If the appointment itself is the frightening part, ask them to come and sit in the waiting room.

Being frightened of a medical appointment at forty or seventy is not childish. Taking someone with you is a practical step, not a confession.

Say the awkward thing when you get there

Worrying about having to explain the gap keeps a lot of people away, so prepare one short sentence and use it. I have not been for years and I was nervous about coming. Or: I am frightened of the dentist and that is why it has been so long.

Said once, it is over, and in almost every case the answer is a version of I am glad you came. It also gets you better care, because they will go slower and tell you what they are doing.

Decide what you will do about the fear of bad news

If the real obstacle is what they might find, it helps to look straight at it. Most of these appointments find nothing. Where something is found, finding it earlier is the whole point of going, and the things screening looks for are far more treatable caught early.

That is not a comforting slogan. It is the actual arithmetic, and it is the opposite of what avoidance assumes.

Then write down the date and treat it as fixed

Once it is booked, put it in the calendar, tell the person who is coming with you, and stop renegotiating it with yourself. The deciding is done. All that is left is going.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor if there is a symptom you have been avoiding having looked at, and say how long it has been without apologizing for it. If fear of needles, dentists, enclosed spaces or examinations is strong enough that you cannot get through the door, tell them that too, since there are good treatments for that specific fear and a therapist can help with it in a small number of sessions. If avoiding things has spread across your life, or you have lost interest in looking after yourself at all, that is worth mentioning to a doctor or a counselor as its own thing.', array['Motivation', 'Shame']::text[], 0, '2026-10-22T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('ordering-something-different-from-everyone-else', 'Ordering Something Different From Everyone Else', 'Saying no to the wine, the shared platter or the dessert can feel like making a scene. How to ask for what you actually need at the table without a speech or an apology.', 'Everyone is ordering. There is a bottle on its way and a plan to share plates, and you need something else: no alcohol, no dairy, no gluten, smaller, plainer, separate, or just not that. And in the half second before you speak, it feels enormous.

So people go along with it. They have the wine they did not want, pick around the thing they cannot eat, say they are not hungry, and spend the evening managing a small problem they could have solved in one sentence at the start.

Why such a small thing feels so loud

Eating together is social glue, and anything that looks like stepping out of the shared thing can feel like a rejection of the group. The fear is not really about the food. It is about being difficult, drawing attention, making the host anxious, or being asked why.

The why is often the worst part. If the reason is private, a medical thing, sobriety, an eating difficulty you are working on, religion, or simply a preference you are tired of defending, you may dread one casual question more than the whole meal.

Short and flat works better than explained

The size of the explanation sets the size of the moment. A long reason invites questions. A short, unbothered sentence closes it.

I will stick with soda water tonight, thanks.

Could I get mine without cheese.

I am going to order my own rather than share, if that is all right.

None for me, thanks. Yes, I am sure.

Say it once, in the same tone you would use to ask for tap water, and then move the conversation on. People follow the lead you give them. If you treat it as unremarkable, it almost always becomes unremarkable.

You do not owe anyone the reason

This is worth saying plainly, because a lot of people believe otherwise. A reason is a courtesy, not a requirement, and no reason is better than a made-up one you will have to keep consistent.

If someone presses, repeat the same thing rather than escalating. Just not tonight, thanks. I am good. It is a long story and I am fine. Repetition is more effective than a better argument, and it is harder to argue with.

Say it earlier than feels necessary

Almost all of the difficulty lives in the first moment. Once the order is placed, the evening is just an evening.

So speak at the start. Tell the host when you accept the invitation, not when you arrive at the table. Tell the server when they take the order rather than waiting to see what arrives. Say it to the group before the bottle is poured rather than letting a full glass sit in front of you all night.

Where it matters medically, be clear rather than polite

A preference can be soft. An allergy, a celiac diagnosis or a serious intolerance cannot, and in a restaurant this is the one place to be plainly direct, because the kitchen needs to know.

I have a serious allergy to nuts, not a preference. Can you check what is in the sauce and whether it is cooked in the same pan.

That is not being difficult. It is giving people the information they need to do their job. Any decent kitchen would rather be told twice than told afterward.

Expect some friction from a few people, and plan the sentence

Most people do not care at all. A few will push, usually because they are uneasy about their own choices, and a couple will take your no personally, especially about alcohol.

Have one line ready for that and do not elaborate. Honestly, I am happier like this. Or: I know, I am no fun. Pass me the bread. Humor ends more of these than firmness does, and you are not obliged to win the exchange, only to finish it.

Sobriety deserves a special mention

If you are not drinking and would rather not discuss it, get a drink in your hand early, since an empty hand draws offers. Order first, so the table takes its lead from you. If pressure is likely, bring a line you have already decided on and tell one person at the table in advance so you are not the only one managing it.

Notice how much easier the next one is

The reason this is worth practicing on small things is that it is the same skill as everything else: asking for a change at work, telling someone what you need, declining an invitation. A table is a low-stakes place to find out that a plain request usually lands without incident, and that evidence transfers.

When to get help

If food in company brings real dread, if you are avoiding meals out, hiding what you eat, or finding that eating in front of people has become one of the harder parts of your week, please talk to your doctor or a therapist, since difficulty around eating responds well to support and is far more common than the silence around it suggests. If not drinking is hard to hold when you are around other people, that is worth saying to a professional too, and there is no shortage of good help for it.', array['Assertiveness', 'Food']::text[], 0, '2026-10-22T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-you-assume-people-do-not-want-you-there', 'When You Assume People Do Not Want You There', 'You hold back from joining in because you are sure you would be imposing. Where that certainty comes from, how to test it instead of believing it, and what it costs to keep guessing.', 'They are going for a drink after work and nobody said your name. The group chat is planning something and you are in the chat. A friend says we should do this again sometime. And in every case you conclude the same thing: they are being polite, you would be an imposition, better not to put yourself where you are not wanted.

So you do not go, do not ask, do not reply. And then the evenings are quiet and the conclusion looks confirmed.

The certainty is the problem, not the question

It would be reasonable to wonder whether you are welcome. What makes this painful is that it does not arrive as a question. It arrives as a fact, fully formed, before any evidence is in.

That jump is called mind reading, and it is one of the most common ways thinking goes wrong. The feeling of certainty is not information about what other people think. It is information about how you expect to be received, which is usually built out of old experiences rather than this group of people.

The second half is the trap

Here is the mechanism that keeps it going. You assume you are not wanted, so you hold back. Holding back looks, from the outside, like not being interested. People invite you less, because they read you as someone who would rather not. That looks to you like proof.

Nobody in this chain is being unkind. Everyone is responding reasonably to what they can see. And the belief gets stronger every round, which is why years can pass like this.

Treat the thought as a guess to test

You cannot argue yourself out of this with positive thinking, and you do not have to. What works is treating the thought as a prediction and finding out.

Write the prediction down specifically before you act. If I ask to join them on Friday, they will hesitate and look awkward. Then do the thing, and afterward write down what actually happened.

Keep the test small. Reply to the group chat with one line. Ask one person what they are doing at the weekend. Say I would like to come, if there is room. Walk over to the table at lunch.

Most people doing this find the result is dull and warm: a yes, a shrug, a nobody-made-anything-of-it. Dull is the outcome you want, and it is more convincing than any amount of reassurance, because you watched it yourself.

Ask instead of guessing

Guessing costs more than asking, which is easy to forget when asking feels so exposed.

Are you two keeping this to yourselves or can anyone come. Is there room for one more. I would like to be included in these if that is all right.

Yes is a good outcome. No, with a reason, is also a good outcome, because it is actual information instead of a story you made up, and it is almost always about numbers or logistics rather than about you.

Separate not invited from not wanted

These are different, and conflating them does a lot of damage.

People invite who they are standing next to, who they messaged last, who came last time, who lives nearby. Most groups form out of habit and convenience and nobody is maintaining a list of who is in and who is out. Being left out of a specific evening is usually evidence of nothing more than how plans get made.

Look at where you learned it

Many people with this pattern can name where it started: being the spare one in a family, being excluded at school, a long stretch of being merely tolerated somewhere. If that was your experience, reading a room for signs you are unwelcome was a sensible skill, and you got good at it.

The trouble is that the skill now fires on people who have given you no cause, and it reads neutral behavior as rejection. Knowing where it came from does not switch it off, and it does make it easier to treat as a habit of mind rather than a perception of the truth.

Let the quiet evidence count

If you have friends who message you, colleagues who save you a seat, family who call, that is evidence. People who assume they are unwanted tend to discount all of it, finding a reason each piece does not count, while a single unanswered message counts completely.

Try holding both. Keep a short note of the times someone sought you out. On the evening when you are certain nobody wants you around, read it.

When to get help

If this is shaping your life, if you are lonely while surrounded by people who might well be glad of you, or if the certainty is heavy enough that testing it feels impossible, this is one of the clearest things to take to a therapist. Checking a belief like this against reality, in small steps with someone alongside you, is standard work in cognitive behavioral therapy and it tends to shift faster than people expect. If the loneliness has turned into low mood that has not lifted in weeks, please tell your doctor as well.', array['CBT', 'Loneliness']::text[], 0, '2026-10-22T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
